{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 174 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "489915e9adf98dca4225b8cdb527800333e913bb8f23f39de8f48a860fa6e710" :edb/db-before-t 173 :edb/db-after-t 174 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533486 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:47:27.138Z" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186059693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401728 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186059693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401735 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186059694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401742 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186059702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401701 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186059702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401708 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186059703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401715 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401694 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186059775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401839 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057000 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186060112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401613 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1071 "$3 \\mid 18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186060112 1071 "$\\,$ $3\\,|\\,18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(3\\,|\\,18\\text{ or }11\\text{ is not prime}) & ≡¬((3\\,|\\,18)∨(11\\text{ is not prime})) \\\\  & ≡¬(3\\,|\\,18)∧¬(11\\text{ is not prime}) \\\\  & ≡(3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18)∧(11\\text{ is prime}) \\\\  & ≡3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18\\text{ and }11\\text{ is prime}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057218 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186060147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401722 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1071 "Given that the minor of $p_{23}$ is $t^{2} + 12$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}t & -8 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186060147 1071 "Given that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1074 "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${p}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t\\cdot 9-(-8)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =9t-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9t-8 & ={t}^{2}+12 \\\\ {t}^{2}-9t+20 & =0 \\\\ (t-5)(t-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=4$ and $t=5.$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057289 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186060156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401749 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1071 "$\\,$ Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1071 "Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186060156 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not water the plants.\n\n- $B:$ The plants will grow.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You water the plants.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, then they will grow.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, they will grow.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186064376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401818 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186064376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401825 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401599 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401846 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186087731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401578 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186087844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401606 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186096620 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401633 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186096790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401804 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098819 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186101253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401654 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1071 "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y + 2x^{2}y^{2}}{x}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*" 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186101253 1071 "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{z}^{-1}+2{x}^{2}{z}^{-2}}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{xz}+\\frac{2x}{{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-2x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098849 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186101258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401660 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + (y(y - 1))/(x) = 0, y(1) = 5, x > 0$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n$\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}z = \\frac{1}{x}$\nusing the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*" 13194139533486 17592186045585 false] [17592186101258 1071 "Solve the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y(y-1)}{x}=0,\\,y(1)=5,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1074 "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{x+C}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{x+C}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition  $y(1)=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{1}{1+C}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{(x-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{(5x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186115197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401811 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186124663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401585 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186129175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401592 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401666 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401673 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152086 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401626 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401619 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401783 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401762 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401769 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401776 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401790 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401797 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401832 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186198261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401680 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186198261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401687 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186198267 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401755 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186198463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401640 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186198463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401647 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401579 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401578 1069 "q-16645" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401578 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401578 1072 #uuid "ba3205c4-c133-5ac7-861e-655b59a14526" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401578 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{1}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =225 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =200 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{200}{9} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{9}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-1-\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)$ and $(-1+\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0).$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401580 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401581 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401582 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401583 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401584 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401579 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401579 1077 "selection" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401579 1079 #edb/ref 17592186401582 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401579 1080 #uuid "d58b31e7-b6c4-5d90-9a58-b8914d59f384" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401580 1081 "There is no $x$-intercept" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401580 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401580 1084 #uuid "0e455adf-7973-5ee4-bb3c-0bd83316580a" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401581 1081 "(1\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401581 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401581 1084 #uuid "d3568acd-90d7-5e24-95a2-7dddb6de4815" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401582 1081 "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401582 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401582 1084 #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401583 1081 "(\\pm \\sqrt{5},0)" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401583 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401583 1084 #uuid "829db562-f4d5-5e31-b45c-b397742cb7e4" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401584 1081 "(1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401584 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401584 1084 #uuid "a69ed72a-53dd-5923-8c05-9efa0f54638b" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401586 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401585 1069 "q-167165" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401585 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16}.$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401585 1072 #uuid "e7d00266-1ded-5f4c-819a-7467528496c3" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401585 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2x,\\,2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401586 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401587 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401586 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401588 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401586 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401589 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401586 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401590 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401586 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401591 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401586 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401586 1077 "selection" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401586 1079 #edb/ref 17592186401588 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401586 1080 #uuid "bc59060d-a9ed-5533-b31b-311227554f8c" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401587 1081 "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=15$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401587 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401587 1084 #uuid "7cd4c18b-e511-5b14-9f13-a5b4cb4f47f2" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401588 1081 "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401588 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401588 1084 #uuid "2821ee27-e28a-5af2-b0bc-7da60bb40070" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401589 1081 "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401589 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401589 1084 #uuid "77dac977-36a1-5c53-9361-88fca32b4abf" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401590 1081 "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=4$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401590 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401590 1084 #uuid "d746e3e5-aa5e-5fa1-88ee-69128529ad71" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401591 1081 "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401591 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401591 1084 #uuid "18156a3d-068d-5e69-8ef7-8771427933c7" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401593 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401592 1069 "q-168921" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401592 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\nII. It is a type II region\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401592 1072 #uuid "fd6f7437-bc45-50cb-9400-bcd422c5b408" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401592 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/bc/bc6b71d1fe69abd34dbd939dc62ede5201b75713e25456cf084e889bc44f449e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.4$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/4a/4a1fd7069abaa3b3fa4d9c286773d226798bf3ad565e85663cab44557fd635c1.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=-1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/26/260e8e959ca95a029a98bd4102106a66df418ea73c0640398c8fdebfdbf812f6.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401593 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401594 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401593 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401595 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401593 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401596 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401593 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401597 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401593 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401598 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401593 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401593 1077 "selection" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401593 1079 #edb/ref 17592186401596 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401593 1080 #uuid "e37c6cf2-31b1-549d-9c9e-1baf99bb11bc" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401594 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401594 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401594 1084 #uuid "50c91312-6d25-5d77-a03c-b9737dcd0907" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401595 1081 "I only" 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401595 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533486 17592186045585 true] [17592186401595 1084 #uuid "3070b0e6-2443-59e9-88ad-5d64b7dbe0c8" 13194